// @@@ If the list is initially not empty, then the result is either null and the last element was null, or the result equals the last element of the list before the operation.
```

We need to produce an assert statement reflecting this. The method is pollLast() returning E (type parameter). The comment: "If the list is initially not empty, then the result is either null and the last element was null, or the result equals the last element of the list before the operation."

Interpretation: For a non-empty list before the call, the result (this method's return value) satisfies: either result is null and the last element before the operation was null, OR result equals the last element before the operation. So basically result == null => old last element was null; else result == old last element. Equivalent: \old(!this.isEmpty()) => ( (\result == null && \old(this.getLast()) == null) || (\result != null && \result.equals(\old(this.getLast()))) ). But we need to use observer methods accessible: we have getLast() method (returns E). There's also maybe peekLast() but getLast() throws if empty; but we are in non-empty case, so ok. Use this.getLast() before operation via \old(this.getLast()). For result null case, we need to compare with null. Use == null. For equality of objects, we might use .equals, but if result is null we can't call equals. So we need condition: (\result == null && \old(this.getLast()) == null) || (\result != null && \result.equals(\old(this.getLast()))). That's fine.

Premise: "If the list is initially not empty". So we need to assert that if \old(!this.isEmpty()) then condition. Use \old(this.isEmpty())? Actually isEmpty() returns true if empty. So not empty means !\old(this.isEmpty()). So assert: !\old(this.isEmpty()) => ( (\result == null && \old(this.getLast()) == null) || (\result != null && \result.equals(\old(this.getLast()))) );

Note that getLast() before operation is safe because list not empty.

